# Ownership points at @leeknowsai and @TracyHQ/super-admin-team.
#
# It pointed at @leeknowsai alone, with a note to switch to a team once one existed,
# because @TracyHQ/registry-maintainers did not. That team still does not, and the
# single point of failure the note warned about arrived: two vendor submissions sat
# with green CI and nowhere to go, and the suggestion on the table was to turn the
# branch protection off entirely.
#
# Widening beats removing. @TracyHQ/super-admin-team already exists and already holds
# nine people who have admin here, @leeknowsai among them, so any of them can review a
# submission and the gate stays up. Move to a narrower registry-maintainers team when
# somebody creates one; that is still the better shape.
#
# This file only gates merges because branch protection on `main` has "Require
# review from Code Owners" turned on. It was turned on the day the repo was
# created, before the first record could be submitted. Without that rule this
# file is advisory, and the sibling MCP registry shipped in exactly that state
# for a while; the consequence there was small because a submission could not
# reach a measured field. Here it can.

# registry/ IS OWNED HERE, AND THAT IS THE DIFFERENCE FROM THE MCP REGISTRY.
#
# There, registry/ is deliberately unowned: platform namespaces are reserved, so
# a submission physically cannot touch a measurement and anyone may merge one.
# Here every record lives under a platform namespace and submissions write into
# the same record the crawler wrote. `src/validate.ts` refuses the measured
# fields, but the rest of the record still needs a human to look at it.
#
# "Anyone can submit" is still true: opening a pull request needs no permission.
# Merging one needs a reader.
/registry/   @leeknowsai @TracyHQ/super-admin-team

/curation/   @leeknowsai @TracyHQ/super-admin-team

# data/ IS DELIBERATELY UNOWNED, AND THAT IS A CHANGE FROM HOW THIS STARTED.
#
# Everything else here is written by a person and needs a reader. data/ is not:
# it is crawler output, regenerated wholesale by a scheduled job that already
# refuses to emit a record failing any of the rules that matter — the served
# path invariant, activeInstalls never travelling without its source, rating
# never without its scale, and no data supplier named in any string. Those
# checks run before the file is written and again in CI before it is served.
#
# Requiring a human to approve a diff of 24,000 machine-generated lines does not
# get that file read. It gets the review clicked through, which is worse than no
# review because it looks like one happened. The Joomla census was merged with
# --admin for exactly this reason, and a gate that is routinely overridden is
# not protecting anything.
#
# NOTE: removing the line below stops data/ needing a CODE OWNER review. It does
# NOT exempt data/ from `required_approving_review_count`, because GitHub cannot
# scope that by path. The scheduled publisher is handled by an explicit bypass
# allowance on the branch protection instead — named, auditable, and limited to
# that identity, rather than the silent "admins can do anything" it relied on
# before.
/schema/     @leeknowsai @TracyHQ/super-admin-team
/src/        @leeknowsai @TracyHQ/super-admin-team
/bin/        @leeknowsai @TracyHQ/super-admin-team
/scripts/    @leeknowsai @TracyHQ/super-admin-team
/.github/    @leeknowsai @TracyHQ/super-admin-team
